            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> It's possible to monkey-patch regular `def` functions so you can't necessarily reason about exactly what's being called. It might be reasonable to assume the same signature though.\n\nI think anyone replacing a function by one with a different signature should be prepared for trouble. It should be safe to assume that a statically declared function signature will not change at runtime. And as long as we only pass it to the type inference, there will be some kind of runtime type check to guard against crashes.\n\n\n> I think the signature would normally describe the wrapped function?\n\nIt should, to satisfy static type checking within the function. But it's not clear who the recipient of the declared signature is. It's programmatically exploitable, so it might even be a hint to a decorator in some cases. Whether that signature is relevant for the resulting function after applying the decorators is unclear, as you noted.\n\nI agree that we should ignore decorated functions if there's at least one non-`@cython…` decorator that we don't know. We could eventually collect a list of known builtin/stdlib decorators that do not alter the signature, but that's not part of a MVP.",
