            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "2 cents from my side:\n\n1. I think all the risks with functions is the same for python global variables. They can be patched and replaced anytime, so I don't see any difference. Even decorator should be reasonable and be implemented to keep function return type:\n   ```python\n   @funny_decorator   # transforms returned str to int\n   def bar() -> str\n        ...\n   a = bar()   # a is actually int\n   ```\n   The function above is actually broken (not sure how it is it managed by type checkers though) and errors from cython should be expected.\n2. I understand cython type inference in a way that inference checks the type **during runtime** and if the type cannot be inferred, it raises an exception. Hence, even the worst case of patching, broken decorators, etc... code ends up as runtime `ValueError`/`TypeError` what is correct behavior - consistent with what is now on master:\n   ```python\n   @cython.cfunc\n   def bar() -> list[int]:\n       return ['3']\n\n   def main():\n       b = bar()\n       c = b[0]\n\n   >>> import a\n   >>> a.main()\n   Traceback (most recent call last):\n     File \"<python-input-1>\", line 1, in <module>\n       a.main()\n       ~~~~~~^^\n     File \"a.py\", line 29, in a.main\n       c = b[0]\n   TypeError: Expected int, got str\n\n\nDo you see any issues with my reasoning? Mainly because of point 2 I am feel comfortable but maybe I miss something... ",
