            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I think all the risks with functions is the same for python global variables. They can be patched and replaced anytime, so I don't see any difference.\n\nI'm not quite sure if it's the same. The annotation `def f(x: int) -> int` could mean one of two things:\n* The name `f` refers to a callable that accepts an `int` and returns an `int` (i.e. equivalent to `f: Callable[[int], int]`,\n* or, this specific callable accepts an `int` and returns an `int` (but it's a property of the callable, not of the name assignment.\n\nThe main thing in its favour, is that I think essentially all type-checkers assume the first option. Probably on the basis that anything else would be so vague that it's useless.\n\n> Even decorator should be reasonable and be implemented to keep function return type: \n\nI definitely don't think this is necessarily true. Both `ty` and `mypy` are happy with\n\n```\nfrom typing import Callable\n\ndef funny_decorator(f: Callable[[], str]) -> Callable[[], int]:\n    return lambda: len(f())\n\n@funny_decorator\ndef bar() -> str:\n     return \"hello\"\n\na = bar()\nprint(a)\na = 1\n```\n\nThat doesn't feel particularly dubious to me.\n\n-----------------------------------------\n\nI think I agree that type inference from `def` functions is reasonable. However I think decorators are explicitly designed to change functions in odd ways, and we should accept that.\n",
